        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Guides",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "How Long Do Portable Power Stations Last",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does a portable power station last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A lithium iron phosphate unit typically lasts around ten years in household use, and an older nickel-based lithium-ion unit three to five. For occasional backup use the limit is calendar ageing rather than the cycle count, because most owners never come close to exhausting the rated cycles.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What does 3,000 cycles mean?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It means the pack can be fully discharged and recharged three thousand times before its capacity falls to eighty per cent of the original. Partial cycles count proportionally, so two half discharges equal one cycle. The unit keeps working past that point; it simply holds less energy.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should I leave my power station plugged in all the time?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Lithium cells age fastest when held at full charge, particularly in a warm room, so permanently topping up a unit for readiness quietly costs you the capacity you were preserving. Store around half charge and top it up every few months instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How should I store a portable power station long term?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "At roughly fifty to sixty per cent charge, somewhere cool and dry, and recharged to that level two or three times a year. Avoid hot garages and unheated sheds in winter, and never leave a unit sitting empty for months — deep discharge can drop cells below recoverable voltage.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does using a power station more often wear it out faster?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Cycling does consume cycle life, but for typical household use the numbers are so generous it barely matters. A pack rated for 3,000 cycles used thirty times a year would last a century on cycles alone, so calendar ageing gets there first and using the unit is not something to avoid.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you replace the battery in a portable power station?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Rarely as a consumer repair. A few manufacturers offer service replacement of the pack, and some larger modular systems use swappable batteries by design, but most units are sealed and the economics usually favour replacement. Check before buying if longevity matters to you.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What kills a power station battery fastest?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Heat, followed by sitting at one hundred per cent charge, followed by charging below freezing. All three are storage decisions rather than usage decisions, which is why two identical units can differ enormously in condition after five years depending on where they were kept.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I know if my power station battery is degrading?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Run a known load and time it. If a lamp that once ran ten hours now runs seven, you have lost roughly thirty per cent of capacity. Other signs are a charge percentage that jumps around under load and a unit that reaches full charge unusually quickly.",
